WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.733
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:09.814
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.114 --> 00:00:16.679
Unit twelve: Consumer & Product News. Today you will explain steps with sequence words and imperatives.

4
00:00:16.979 --> 00:00:19.550
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.652
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:25.704
Product recall.

7
00:00:26.717 --> 00:00:30.806
Product recall means an order to return a product because it may be unsafe.

8
00:00:31.417 --> 00:00:34.078
A product recall removed the toy from shops.

9
00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:37.649
Your turn. Say: Product recall.

10
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.000
Product recall.

11
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.215
Consumer protection.

12
00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:49.260
Consumer protection means laws that keep buyers safe from unfair business.

13
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.057
Consumer protection laws stop false adverts.

14
00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:56.999
Your turn. Say: Consumer protection.

15
00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:01.310
Consumer protection.

16
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.016
Warranty.

17
00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:07.479
Warranty means a promise to repair or replace a faulty product.

18
00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:10.083
The warranty lasts for two years.

19
00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:13.045
Your turn. Say: Warranty.

20
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:16.924
Warranty.

21
00:01:17.683 --> 00:01:18.998
Safety standard.

22
00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:24.142
Safety standard means an official rule about how safe a product must be.

23
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.144
The helmet meets every safety standard.

24
00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.739
Your turn. Say: Safety standard.

25
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.147
Safety standard.

26
00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:37.047
Market launch.

27
00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.025
Market launch means the start of selling a new product.

28
00:01:41.617 --> 00:01:43.651
The market launch is planned for May.

29
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.053
Your turn. Say: Market launch.

30
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.075
Market launch.

31
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:53.189
Price increase.

32
00:01:54.183 --> 00:01:57.239
Price increase means a rise in the cost of something.

33
00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.511
A price increase surprised many shoppers.

34
00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:03.943
Your turn. Say: Price increase.

35
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.207
Price increase.

36
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.323
Customer complaint.

37
00:02:11.317 --> 00:02:15.290
Customer complaint means a statement that a buyer is unhappy with a product.

38
00:02:15.883 --> 00:02:18.185
Each customer complaint was recorded.

39
00:02:18.783 --> 00:02:21.842
Your turn. Say: Customer complaint.

40
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.189
Customer complaint.

41
00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:28.683
Regulatory agency.

42
00:02:29.683 --> 00:02:34.446
Regulatory agency means an official body that makes sure companies follow rules.

43
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.676
The regulatory agency tested the product.

44
00:02:38.283 --> 00:02:41.655
Your turn. Say: Regulatory agency.

45
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.233
Regulatory agency.

46
00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:50.928
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:51.783 --> 00:02:56.015
Visal, a company has announced a product recall. What should buyers do?

48
00:02:57.117 --> 00:03:06.680
First, stop using the product. Next, check the model number. Then contact the company, and finally return it for a refund.

49
00:03:07.783 --> 00:03:10.096
You used lots of sequence words.

50
00:03:11.183 --> 00:03:20.976
Yes: first, next, then, after that, finally. Consumer news uses them with imperatives such as 'check', 'stop' and 'keep your receipt'.

51
00:03:22.083 --> 00:03:24.524
Who makes sure companies follow the rules?

52
00:03:25.617 --> 00:03:32.832
A regulatory agency. It sets the safety standard, and a customer complaint can start an investigation.

53
00:03:33.967 --> 00:03:35.467
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:36.317 --> 00:03:39.617
Grammar focus: Sequence words and imperatives.

55
00:03:39.917 --> 00:03:40.976
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:41.683 --> 00:03:43.578
First, stop using the product.

57
00:03:44.483 --> 00:03:46.309
Next, check the model number.

58
00:03:47.217 --> 00:03:49.379
Finally, return it for a refund.

59
00:03:50.333 --> 00:03:57.583
Use first, next, then, after that and finally to order steps. Imperatives begin with the base verb: check, stop, keep.

60
00:03:58.317 --> 00:04:02.246
Be careful! Don't say: First, you are check the label.

61
00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.490
Say: First, check the label.

62
00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:08.929
An imperative uses the base verb.

63
00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:12.444
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:14.908
What To Do After a Recall.

65
00:04:15.208 --> 00:04:19.053
A product recall means a company believes its product may be unsafe.

66
00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:23.810
First, stop using it. Next, check the model number on the label.

67
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.959
Then read the company's notice, and contact it if you have a customer complaint.

68
00:04:29.967 --> 00:04:35.496
Finally, return the product, because the warranty and consumer protection rules often give you a refund.

69
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:41.135
A regulatory agency may also publish the safety standard that was broken.

70
00:04:42.233 --> 00:04:46.305
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:49.091
blank, stop using the product.

72
00:04:49.391 --> 00:04:52.917
A, First. B, Finally. C, Lastly then.

73
00:04:56.467 --> 00:04:58.222
The answer is A: First.

74
00:04:59.383 --> 00:05:02.788
An order to return a possibly unsafe product is a blank.

75
00:05:03.088 --> 00:05:06.474
A, warranty. B, recall. C, launch.

76
00:05:10.033 --> 00:05:11.766
The answer is B: recall.

77
00:05:12.917 --> 00:05:15.857
A promise to repair a faulty product is a blank.

78
00:05:16.157 --> 00:05:19.682
A, warranty. B, complaint. C, agency.

79
00:05:23.233 --> 00:05:25.465
The answer is A: warranty.

80
00:05:26.617 --> 00:05:28.558
Choose the correct imperative.

81
00:05:28.858 --> 00:05:34.056
A, You are check the number.. B, Check the model number.. C, Checking you the number..

82
00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.424
The answer is B: Check the model number..

83
00:05:41.583 --> 00:05:44.361
According to the reading, buyers often receive a…

84
00:05:44.661 --> 00:05:48.546
A, fine. B, refund. C, ticket.

85
00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.088
The answer is B: refund.

86
00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.521
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:59.383 --> 00:06:02.811
What should a consumer do first after hearing about a product recall?

88
00:06:11.617 --> 00:06:12.943
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:13.243 --> 00:06:34.209
The first thing a consumer should do is stop using the product immediately, because it may be dangerous. After that, they should check the model number and read the official notice. If it is affected, they should contact the company for a refund or repair. Keeping the receipt is also useful.

90
00:06:34.509 --> 00:06:36.416
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:37.317 --> 00:06:42.001
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and sequence words and imperatives.

92
00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:46.091
Today's value is safety. Be careful near cars and roads.

93
00:06:46.391 --> 00:06:49.322
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:49.622 --> 00:06:52.205
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

